{"date":"2026-09-18","heading":"September 18, 2026","editions":[{"edition":"recap","byline":"Yesterday in review","text":"September 17 added up to one of the busiest days of the week, with 302 feeder visits and 21 species recorded against a seven-day average of roughly 170 visits. Tufted Titmice remained the dominant feeder presence, accounting for 123 of the visits, while Carolina Chickadees and Ruby-throated Hummingbirds also held steady as regulars.\n\nThe acoustic record was dominated by Carolina Wrens, which contributed 132 detections, and Northern Cardinals, with 39. Several other species, including Blue Jays and Gray Catbirds, were heard frequently, while rare visitors like the Red-shouldered Hawk and White-eyed Vireo appeared briefly. All ten species that appear daily at this station were recorded again.\n\nActivity at the station shifted following the installation of a new seed feeder earlier in the week, which replaced a unit previously causing detection problems. This change aligns with the rise in visual records, where Tufted Titmice and Carolina Chickadees filled the majority of visits.","time":"10:09"},{"edition":"morning","byline":"Dawn patrol","text":"The morning opened with a sparse acoustic chorus, led by the Carolina Wren and Northern Mockingbird before sunrise. By 07:17, the American Crow and Carolina Chickadee joined the early calls. The station has remained unusually quiet since, with only one probable visual record of a Tufted Titmouse at 10:14.\n\nThe Carolina Wren dominated the 08:00 hour with 42 detections, while Northern Cardinals and Tufted Titmice also registered in the sound. No other species has made a confirmed visit to the feeder in over two hours.","time":"10:30"}],"sunrise":"07:17","sunset":"19:34","weather":{"high_c":19.389,"wind_kph":1.0826363636363636,"precip_mm":0.0,"hourly":["clear","clear","clear","clear","clear","clear","clear","clear","clear","partly","clear",null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null]